WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.794
Machine learning promises to accelerate therapeutic
antibody design,

00:00:04.874 --> 00:00:10.070
but only if models can accurately read the
sequence-to-function fitness landscape.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.251
This paper introduces FLAb, the Fitness Landscape
for Antibodies,

00:00:14.331 --> 00:00:17.532
the largest therapeutic antibody benchmark to date.

00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:23.129
It gathers experimental measurements for six
developability properties and uses them to

00:00:23.209 --> 00:00:28.725
stress-test six widely used deep learning protein
models against physics-based Rosetta.

00:00:28.805 --> 00:00:34.515
The headline finding is sobering: no single model
correlates well with all six properties,

00:00:34.595 --> 00:00:38.889
and performance swings sharply across datasets of
the same property,

00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:41.720
revealing how far the field still has to go.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.875
Machine learning can only speed up antibody design
if models truly understand what makes an antibody good.

00:00:48.955 --> 00:00:53.272
Yet the major protein fitness benchmarks, like
CAFA, TAPE, and FLIP,

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:57.864
either leave antibody data out entirely or include
only a sliver of it.

00:00:57.944 --> 00:01:03.360
That leaves researchers with no principled way to
check whether a deep learning model

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.624
actually captures antibody fitness.

00:01:05.704 --> 00:01:10.927
Today the field often falls back on weak proxies
such as native sequence recovery,

00:01:11.007 --> 00:01:14.160
which say little about real therapeutic potential.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:19.789
A therapeutic antibody has to satisfy many demands
at once: it must express well,

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:24.658
stay stable, avoid triggering an immune response,
bind its target tightly,

00:01:24.738 --> 00:01:29.132
resist aggregation, and steer clear of sticking to
the wrong things.

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:34.527
These properties often pull against each other, so
improving one can hurt another.

00:01:34.607 --> 00:01:39.001
If a model could reliably score candidates across
all of these axes,

00:01:39.081 --> 00:01:42.553
it could replace slow and expensive wet-lab screening.

00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:47.422
As new antibody design methods keep appearing, the
field needs one shared,

00:01:47.502 --> 00:01:50.580
antibody-focused benchmark to judge them fairly.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.051
To fill this gap, the authors build FLAb, the
Fitness Landscape for Antibodies,

00:01:56.131 --> 00:02:00.436
the largest therapeutic antibody design benchmark
assembled so far.

00:02:00.516 --> 00:02:07.636
It curates seventeen mutational landscapes with
more than thirteen thousand experimental fitness measurements,

00:02:07.716 --> 00:02:10.189
spanning six developability properties.

00:02:10.269 --> 00:02:11.564
Using this benchmark,

00:02:11.644 --> 00:02:18.175
they evaluate six widely adopted pretrained protein
models and compare them to physics-based Rosetta.

00:02:18.255 --> 00:02:19.484
Just as importantly,

00:02:19.564 --> 00:02:24.720
all of the FLAb data are released openly so the
community can keep expanding it.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.580
The core idea is simple.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.546
Every antibody sequence, or its structure, is fed
to a model,

00:02:30.626 --> 00:02:36.918
and the model reports a perplexity score averaged
over all residues in the heavy and light chains.

00:02:36.998 --> 00:02:41.014
Perplexity measures how surprised the model is by
the sequence,

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:45.435
so a well-behaved model should be confident,
meaning low perplexity,

00:02:45.515 --> 00:02:47.385
about high-fitness antibodies.

00:02:47.465 --> 00:02:52.911
The authors then correlate these scores against
real experimental fitness using three

00:02:52.991 --> 00:02:55.512
coefficients: Pearson for linear trends,

00:02:55.592 --> 00:02:59.998
Spearman for monotonic trends, and Kendall tau for
ordinal agreement.

00:03:00.078 --> 00:03:05.394
Crucially, the models are used exactly as released,
with no additional fine-tuning,

00:03:05.474 --> 00:03:09.880
across decoder-only, encoder-only, and
inverse-folding architectures.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.562
FLAb pulls together seventeen mutational landscapes
from distinct antibody families,

00:03:15.642 --> 00:03:17.740
drawn from eight separate studies,

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.712
adding up to more than thirteen thousand fitness
measurements.

00:03:21.792 --> 00:03:28.183
Together they cover the six developability
properties that define a therapeutic antibody: expression,

00:03:28.263 --> 00:03:33.500
thermostability, immunogenicity, aggregation,
polyreactivity, and binding affinity.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.345
Each property is grounded in its own real
experimental unit,

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:42.854
from micrograms per milliliter for expression to
melting temperature for stability and

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:45.097
dissociation constants for binding.

00:03:45.177 --> 00:03:52.080
This diversity is what lets FLAb test whether a
model generalizes across the many facets of antibody fitness.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:56.225
The headline result is a reality check.

00:03:56.305 --> 00:04:00.070
No model correlates well with all six properties,
and even for a single property,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.915
performance swings widely from one dataset to another.

00:04:03.995 --> 00:04:07.760
ProGen2-Small was the most frequent winner, coming
out on top for seven datasets,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.605
while ProGen2-Medium, ProGen2-OAS, ESM-IF,

00:04:11.685 --> 00:04:15.450
and Rosetta energy tied just behind at six each.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.295
Notably,

00:04:19.375 --> 00:04:23.140
sequence-based models on average beat
structure-based ones across every landscape,

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.985
with the biggest gap on thermostability,

00:04:27.065 --> 00:04:30.830
suggesting that raw sequence signal still carries
most of the predictive power here.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.544
Digging into what drives performance,

00:04:33.624 --> 00:04:39.154
the authors find that parameter count matters more
than architecture or training data.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:42.090
Encoder-only AntiBERTy and decoder-only IgLM,

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:47.113
both trained on the same five hundred fifty eight
million antibody sequences,

00:04:47.193 --> 00:04:48.810
behave almost identically.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:55.855
Scaling ProGen2 from one hundred fifty million up
to over six billion parameters helped only two properties,

00:04:55.935 --> 00:04:58.139
polyreactivity and thermostability.

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:04.140
They also uncover an evolutionary bias: several
language models rank the wild-type golimumab

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:08.577
antibody as fitter than mutants that are actually
more thermostable,

00:05:08.657 --> 00:05:11.904
while physics-based Rosetta gets the ranking right.

00:05:11.984 --> 00:05:17.710
It's a reminder that evolutionary likelihood and
physical fitness are not the same thing.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.215
A few numbers capture the scope and the findings.

00:05:21.295 --> 00:05:27.233
FLAb spans thirteen thousand three hundred eighty
four fitness measurements across seventeen

00:05:27.313 --> 00:05:30.896
mutational landscapes and six developability properties,

00:05:30.976 --> 00:05:34.102
testing six deep learning models against Rosetta.

00:05:34.182 --> 00:05:38.092
Intrinsic properties, the ones driven by the
antibody itself,

00:05:38.172 --> 00:05:41.951
reach an average absolute correlation above zero
point six,

00:05:42.031 --> 00:05:47.119
while extrinsic properties lag well behind, with
binding under zero point four,

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:52.286
expression under zero point four two, and
immunogenicity under zero point five.

00:05:52.366 --> 00:05:56.800
And models are far better at telling apart mutants
within one family,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.594
at zero point seven seven, than across different families,

00:06:00.674 --> 00:06:02.360
at just zero point one two.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:08.307
The takeaway is honest and useful: no current deep
learning model reliably predicts antibody

00:06:08.387 --> 00:06:11.069
fitness across all developability properties,

00:06:11.149 --> 00:06:17.330
so we are not yet at the point of trusting these
models to filter therapeutic candidates on their own.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:22.241
The good news is that intrinsic properties are
already captured reasonably well,

00:06:22.321 --> 00:06:25.494
which points toward the hardest remaining challenges.

00:06:25.574 --> 00:06:31.878
The authors argue the most promising path forward
is to enrich these models with structural information,

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:34.456
antigen context, and physics-based priors,

00:06:34.536 --> 00:06:38.200
and to keep growing open antibody fitness datasets
like FLAb.
